# External-service call sizing and timeouts

When a pipeline that already batches for internal reasons (DB writes, file
I/O) also calls out to an external service (HTTP, RPC, etc.) once per batch,
do not silently reuse the internal batch-size constant as the payload size
for that external call. They are unrelated concerns sized for different
things — one for local write efficiency, one for what a remote service can
process inside its response-time budget — and coupling them means a later
change to the internal batch size (tuned for the DB) silently changes the
external call's payload size and failure risk too.

1. **Size the external call for the external service**, not for whatever
   internal batch size happens to be in scope. If the service's realistic
   processing time for the largest payload you intend to send is not known,
   chunk the call (send it in bounded sub-batches) rather than sending the
   full internal batch in one request.
2. **Set the timeout to fit the payload size you actually send**, and treat
   the two as one tunable pair, not independent defaults. A fixed timeout
   sized for a small/typical payload will misclassify a *slow-but-alive*
   service (busy processing a large, legitimate request) as an outage.
3. **Scope the failure to the call, not the whole job**, where practical. A
   single oversized external call timing out should not be indistinguishable
   from a genuine service outage if a smaller call would have succeeded —
   prefer retry/chunk-and-retry for a timeout over failing an entire
   multi-batch job on the first slow response.
